    "modelAnswer": "Both endings reflect the values of their time to a great extent: Casablanca resolves private desire into wartime duty, whereas Bonnie and Clyde ends with the state destroying its young rebels.\n\nIn Casablanca, Rick sends Ilsa away with Laszlo in a studio fog lit with hard backlight, and Steiner’s “La Marseillaise” swells as he walks off with Renault towards “a beautiful friendship”: complete classical closure in which the individual is reintegrated into the collective cause of 1942. Bonnie and Clyde refuses that closure. Birds scatter, the lovers exchange a look in rapid close-ups, and slow-motion gunfire is followed by silence and a cut to black. For a 1967 audience watching Vietnam on television, the ending reads as the establishment’s violence against the counterculture. The contrast is not just of story but of form, as continuity and score give way to elliptical editing and silence."
