    "modelAnswer": "Visual and soundtrack cues are central to spectator response in both films, though their effect depends on what different spectators bring to them.\n\nIn Get Out’s hypnosis sequence, the chime of Missy’s spoon against the teacup becomes a sound cue that triggers both Chris’s paralysis and the spectator’s dread. As he sinks into the Sunken Place, subjective camera and slow falling motion show him watching the world through a distant screen while the sound becomes muffled, so the spectator shares his powerlessness through form. Peele has described the Sunken Place as a metaphor for the marginalisation of Black people, and Black spectators may recognise this immediately, an example of hooks’ oppositional gaze. The cues are therefore highly important, but their meaning is completed by the spectator’s identity and context."
