    "modelAnswer": "Ideology shapes response strongly in Get Out, whose critique of liberal racism is precise, but more ambiguously in Joker, whose class politics divide spectators.\n\nGet Out’s garden party turns ideology into image: white guests inspect Chris’s body and praise his physique, and the later silent bingo-card auction, intercut so the spectator understands before Chris, evokes a slave auction. Dramatic irony positions the spectator against the Armitages’ smiling liberalism. Joker’s Murray Franklin sequence is less certain. Cross-cutting between the studio murder and the burning streets presents violence as the product of class neglect, yet the triumphant framing of Arthur on the car bonnet allows a preferred reading of sympathy and an oppositional reading of alarm (Hall). Ideology therefore shapes response in both, but Joker’s ambiguity is itself the reason its spectators respond so differently."
