    "modelAnswer": "An ideological approach is highly useful for both resolutions, because each ending shows consumer capitalism absorbing rebellion, although comedy and irony limit any single reading.\n\nTrainspotting’s resolution inverts its opening. Renton steals the drug money while his friends sleep and walks towards the camera as “Born Slippy” builds, while his voiceover promises to be “just like you: the job, the family, the big television”. The bookended “Choose Life” monologue, once a rejection of consumer conformity, now becomes its acceptance, so the narrative rewards Thatcherite individualism over collective loyalty. An ideological reading explains why the ending feels both a betrayal and a triumph. However, Renton leaves money for Spud and his tone is ironic, so the approach is very useful but not sufficient alone."
