    "modelAnswer": "An ideological approach is very useful because, in both films, the central binary oppositions turn out to be false: heroin and consumerism, the living and the undead.\n\nShaun of the Dead sets up the opposition of living and undead only to collapse it. Shaun’s two walks to the corner shop are filmed as matching long handheld tracking shots along the same route; the second time zombies and blood fill the street and he notices nothing. Lévi-Strauss’s alive/dead binary seems to structure the genre narrative, but a Marxist reading shows the film’s real point: alienating routine has already made Shaun one of the living dead. The ending confirms this when zombies are absorbed as supermarket labour and daytime-TV entertainment, so ideology explains why the opposition dissolves."
