    "modelAnswer": "In both films mise-en-scène turns setting into argument: in Pan’s Labyrinth the fantasy mirrors fascism, while in City of God the changing favela shows a cycle of violence created by state neglect.\n\nThe Pale Man’s lair is the clearest example in Pan’s Labyrinth. A banquet sits untouched before a motionless, eyeless figure, the walls are painted with murals of him devouring children, and a shallow-focus close-up of a pile of small shoes evokes Holocaust imagery. The design deliberately mirrors Vidal’s earlier dinner for the priest and officials, so the monster becomes an image of a Church and state that gorge themselves while crushing the innocent. When Ofelia disobeys and eats two grapes, the mise-en-scène aligns her disobedience with the rebels’ resistance, proving that the fantasy is political rather than escapist."
